---
import RwLayout from '../../layouts/RwLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const sections = [
  { slug: 'previous-runs', text: 'The Previous Runs API' },
  { slug: 'era5-reference', text: 'ERA5 via the Archive API' },
  { slug: 'response-anatomy', text: 'Anatomy of a response' },
  { slug: 'verified-behaviors', text: 'Verified behaviors' },
  { slug: 'good-citizen', text: 'Being a good citizen' },
];
---

<RwLayout
  title="Data primer — Reference Workflow"
  description="What the Open-Meteo Previous Runs and Archive APIs actually return: lead semantics, units, missing values, and the behaviors worth knowing before your agent assumes otherwise."
  current="data-primer"
  sections={sections}
>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Data primer</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-gray-600">
      Background on the two APIs the exercise uses, for when you want the details.
    </p>

    <article class="prose prose-gray max-w-none mt-8">
      <p>
        The exercise uses two public <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a> endpoints:
        one that serves each model's forecasts <em>as they were issued</em>, and one that serves
        the ERA5 reanalysis the forecasts are scored against. Everything below is something your
        agent will otherwise assume; the dated items at the end are behaviors verified directly
        against the live API.
      </p>

      <h2 id="previous-runs">Forecasts: the Previous Runs API</h2>
      <p>
        <code>https://previous-runs-api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast</code> returns, for each
        requested variable, one series per forecast lead. Appending
        <code>_previous_day0</code> through <code>_previous_day7</code> to a variable name selects
        the run: for a given valid hour, <code>temperature_2m_previous_day2</code> is what the
        model's run from two days earlier predicted for that same hour. <code>previous_day0</code>
        is the most recent run's value. Runs are archived from January 2024 onward.
      </p>
      <p>A worked example (one point on the Colorado Front Range, one model, one lead):</p>
      <pre><code>https://previous-runs-api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast
    ?latitude=40.0&amp;longitude=-105.0
    &amp;start_date=2026-08-31&amp;end_date=2026-09-06
    &amp;hourly=temperature_2m_previous_day1
    &amp;models=gfs_global
    &amp;timezone=GMT</code></pre>
      <p>The exercise's four models, chosen to pair two kinds of forecasting:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Model parameter</th><th>What it is</th><th>Kind</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>gfs_global</code></td><td>NOAA GFS</td><td>Physics-based (NWP)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ecmwf_ifs025</code></td><td>ECMWF IFS, 0.25°</td><td>Physics-based (NWP)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>gfs_graphcast025</code></td><td>GraphCast, GFS-initialized</td><td>Machine-learning</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ecmwf_aifs025_single</code></td><td>ECMWF AIFS, single</td><td>Machine-learning</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="era5-reference">The reference: ERA5 via the Archive API</h2>
      <p>
        <code>https://archive-api.open-meteo.com/v1/archive</code> serves the ERA5 reanalysis:
        global, 0.25°, hourly, from 1940 to a few days ago. Two facts about it shape the whole
        exercise:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It runs about six days behind the present.</strong> On 2026-09-29, the latest
          ERA5 hour available was 2026-09-23 23:00 UTC. The lag belongs to the reference, not the
          forecast models, and it is the one hard constraint on your window: the exercise's
          default ends at least eight days back, which leaves a margin. Live dates fetch
          forecasts; they cannot yet verify them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is a reanalysis, not observations.</strong> ERA5 is a model-data blend with
          error characteristics of its own: for some variables, in some terrain, appreciable
          ones. Every statistic in this exercise is an error relative to ERA5, and the prompts and
          pages here say "reference," never "truth," because the difference is real.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="response-anatomy">Anatomy of a response</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Hourly data arrives as <strong>parallel arrays</strong>: one <code>time</code> array,
          and one value array per variable/lead, index-aligned. Gaps are <code>null</code> entries
          <em>inside</em> the arrays; array lengths stay equal.
        </li>
        <li>
          A <code>hourly_units</code> object accompanies the data, naming the unit of every
          series. Record it; task 2's unit assertions are fed from it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Times are ISO 8601 without a zone suffix, interpreted in the requested
          <code>timezone</code>. The API defaults to GMT; the exercise requests
          <code>timezone=GMT</code> explicitly so the intent is on the record. (The task-1
          episode note describes what omitting this parameter cost the original build.)
        </li>
        <li>
          Default units are metric: <strong>°C, km/h, mm</strong>. The exercise fetches at
          defaults and converts to report units (°F, mph, inches) once, at the reporting
          boundary, so the conversion exists as code that can carry assertions rather than as a
          query parameter.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="verified-behaviors">Verified behaviors</h2>
      <p>
        Three behaviors confirmed against the live API while this page was drafted, each with the
        date it was checked. Any of them may change; the date is part of the fact.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>A model can return nothing at all, and the response still looks
          normal.</strong> On 2026-09-29, <code>gfs_graphcast025</code> returned all-null arrays
          for every variable at every lead, in every window tried back to June 2026, with a
          perfectly ordinary <code>hourly_units</code> entry attached; the same request against
          <code>ecmwf_aifs025_single</code> returned complete series. (On 2026-09-14 the pattern
          was different: the machine-learning models' wind series came back empty.) A series with
          no values produces no matched pairs, so its statistics are unavailable. Record that; do
          not let an agent fill the series or drop it silently.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>bounding_box</code> is not supported for every model</strong>
          (2026-09-14). A box
          request with <code>models=gfs_global</code> returns an error
          (<code>"Bounding box calls not supported for domain gfs_global"</code>); the same
          request against <code>ecmwf_ifs025</code> succeeds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A bounding-box response is an array of per-gridpoint objects, and nothing in
          any object names the model that produced it.</strong> Each element carries its own
          latitude and longitude; attribution to a model exists only in your request. If you fetch
          a box for several models, your bookkeeping is the only thing connecting responses to
          models. State that in your plan and assert it in your code.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If you want a box rather than a point, the practical shape on 2026-09-14:
        <code>bounding_box=39,-106,41,-104</code> (south, west, north, east); either drop
        <code>gfs_global</code> or fetch it per-point. A single point sidesteps all three
        behaviors, and the exercise loses nothing.
      </p>

      <h2 id="good-citizen">Being a good citizen</h2>
      <p>
        Open-Meteo's free tier is for non-commercial use with attribution, and the exercise's
        fetches are small: a handful of requests, once, with the raw responses saved and reused.
        An agent re-fetching on every run instead of reading its saved files deserves a note in
        your review, and is the usual way rate limits get hit.
      </p>

      <p>
        Back to <a href={`${base}reference-workflow/task-1/`}>task 1</a>, or on to the
        <a href={`${base}reference-workflow/statistics/`}>statistics appendix</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
</RwLayout>
